title: timeout is accepted but never applied to the async transport - a stalled crawl
  never resolves
updated: 2026-10-13T19:50:22Z
---

`timeout` is one of the keys forwarded to the internal `WWW::Crawl4AI` (`lib/Net/Async/Crawl4AI.pm:91-94`), where it configures the LWP user agent of the sync client (default 120 s). The async transport is built without it: `http` creates `Net::Async::HTTP->new(user_agent => ..., max_connections_per_host => 4)` (`Crawl4AI.pm:149-158`), and `_do_request_with_retry` calls `do_request(request => ..., fail_on_error => 0)` with no `timeout` either (`Crawl4AI.pm:210`). Net::Async::HTTP has no default timeout.

Effect: `Net::Async::Crawl4AI->new(timeout => 30)` is silently ignored; a Crawl4AI container that accepts the request and hangs (browser stuck, the common failure of this service) leaves the Future of `crawl`/`markdown`/`screenshot` pending forever, and with it the strategy chain, which never escalates to the next backend. The retry policy cannot help because no failure is ever produced.

Done when: the default `http` gets `timeout => $self->client->timeout` (and/or each `do_request` passes it), a timeout surfaces as the `transport` error and is retried like other transport failures, and a test with a never-resolving mock http proves it.

